From: DockSeed <dockseed@proton.me>
Subject: [PATCH] drm: allwinner: A733 DP -- the main video stream, computed from the mode

[Recipe cut: regenerated: context only, the removed debugfs knobs of earlier
patches are not touched here.]

The transmitter's half of the pixel path: the Main Stream Attributes, the
transfer unit, the second register window's clock gates, and the two enables
that start the stream. With `drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source`'s TCON feeding it and link training done by
hand, this is what a monitor turns into a picture.

THE MSA BLOCK IS COMPUTABLE, AND IS COMPUTED
---------------------------------------------

Until 2026-08-19 the transfer unit was carried from a vendor capture and
SRC0_USER_DATA_CNT was a register found by diffing our window against that
capture. Both are derivable: UM 15.4.5.3 states the
equations, and re-deriving them reproduces what a live vendor link carries at
1920x1200 over four lanes, to the digit:

    LinkBW      = LINK_BW_SET * 27 * LANE_COUNT_SET    0x0A * 27 * 4 = 1080
    Data Rate   = pixel clock * bpp / 8                154 * 24 / 8  =  462
    TU symbols  = Data Rate / LinkBW * TU_SIZE         462/1080 * 64 = 27.3778
                                                       -> 27 + 6/16 = 0x061B0040
    SYMBOL_COUNT    = (HRES * bpp + 7) / 8             (1920*24+7)/8 = 5760
    USER_DATA_COUNT = (SYMBOL_COUNT + lanes - 1)/lanes (5760+3)/4    = 1440

⚠ AND THE POINT OF COMPUTING IS THE LANE COUNT. The by-hand script hardcodes
the four-lane words, and they are wrong for two lanes -- which wants
0x0C360040 and 0xB40 -- so two-lane runs that used those words and looked
clean were running an under-programmed transfer unit. This driver never sees
a constant, so it cannot make that mistake.

The tidy explanation for why two lanes tolerated a four-lane TU is eliminated,
not assumed: SRC0_USER_CONTROL bit 0 USER_SPARSE_MODE_ENABLE would make the core
ignore SYMBOLS_PER_TU entirely, and it reads ZERO on the
vendor's live link. Sparse is off and the symbol count is honoured. This driver
does not turn it on and says why -- it would paper over exactly the arithmetic
this patch exists to get right -- and its read-out warns if anything else has.

⚠ Field decode, UM 15.4.7.56: [15:0] TU_SIZE, [23:16] SYMBOLS_PER_TU, [27:24]
FRAC_SYMBOLS_PER_TU in sixteenths. The UM contradicts itself on the middle one --
15.4.5.3's prose puts it at [22:16], the register table at
[23:16] -- and it cannot matter, because the field's own maximum is 64. Both are
cited; neither alone.

TU_SIZE stays at the register's reset 64, the top of the 32..64 range the UM
allows, and the manual mode is chosen deliberately: the automatic mode
(SYMBOLS_PER_TU = 0) may not suit every sink, and the UM advises the manual
mode for boxes that must work with a wide range of sinks. A monitor on a
Type-C cable is exactly that.

SRC0_USER_DATA_CNT IS NOT OPTIONAL, AND HERE IS WHY
----------------------------------------------------

🔴 Left at its reset zero, it is what stood between a fully trained link and
a picture. The register is the per-lane symbol count of one line of active
video; the framer sends that many from the user FIFO and then starts
blanking (15.4.7.59) -- so at zero the blanking start comes after zero
symbols of active video and every line is blank. What that looked like on
2026-08-18: all four lanes CR and EQ and symbol-locked, interlane aligned,
zero symbol errors on every lane, the sink reporting itself in sync,
SRC0_USER_FIFO_STATUS clean -- and the monitor not even waking from standby.
It was found by diffing this 4 KiB window against a vendor live-link
capture, 70 non-zero words on each side, and it was the one register the
vendor sets and we did not.

That also half-answers a question that was still open: which of
SRC0_USER_DATA_CNT and TV_PIXELDEPTH_MODE was load-bearing, given both were
written in the same minute. USER_DATA_CNT is settled from the doc alone --
at zero it cannot produce active video, whatever else is right.
TV_PIXELDEPTH is not: at its reset value TCON_TV1 runs in 6-bit mode (the
6-bit colorbar, if the colorbar is its source), into a framing configuration
counting 24 bits per pixel, which is provably INCONSISTENT with MISC0 but
not provably fatal. That half needs one write on a live link and is in the
acceptance list below.

SRC0_SECONDARY_DATA_WINDOW -- THE ONE DELIBERATE DEPARTURE
-----------------------------------------------------------

We write what the UM's equation gives, 252, where both our by-hand run and the
vendor write 98. The decision, and the reasoning, because it is the only value
in this patch that a working link disagrees with:

  - The UM gives a hard rule and a recommendation. The rule is that the value
    must stay below the horizontal blanking period, counted in main link
    symbols; the recommendation is the formula Reg = HBLANK_PERIOD /
    LINK_SYMBOL_CLOCK_PERIOD * 0.9 (15.4.7.40). For this mode the blanking
    period is 280 link symbol clocks and the recommendation is 252. Both 98 and
    252 satisfy the rule.
  - 98 is not two independent sources agreeing. The vendor computes it as
    HSTART * (link symbol rate / 2) / pixel clock (trilinear_dp14.c:889-903) --
    112 * 135000 / 154000 = 98 -- from an undocumented formula over the wrong
    quantity, HSTART rather than the blanking period, with a bare /2 that
    hardcodes a symbols-per-link-clock ratio. Our by-hand value was copied off
    that live link. One source, seen twice.
  - This tree's rule is that a value comes from the reference docs and that
    vendor code is a hint to verify. The doc has an equation, and 98 does not
    satisfy it.

Reverting costs one write on a live link and no rebuild: `devmem 0x05740808 32
0x62`. Falsified by: SRC0_USER_FIFO_STATUS or SRC0_USER_FRAMING_STATUS going
non-zero, or the picture going away.

SRC0_DATA_CONTROL IS NOT WRITTEN, BUT IT IS CHECKED
----------------------------------------------------

The UM tells integrators to leave this register alone unless the vendor's
support engineers direct otherwise (15.4.7.42), so it is left alone -- and
its reset value 0x00002004 sits exactly on the one constraint the UM does
impose: 15.4.5.4 requires DATA_ACCUMULATION_DELAY to be no smaller than half
the TU size, and the reset is a delay of 32 against a TU size of 64.
Anything that raises TU_SIZE or lowers the delay breaks it, and the symptom
would be uneven spacing of the Blanking Start symbols, not an error. So the
check takes the TU size as an argument and refuses, rather than trusting
that nobody will ever change the constant.

MVID AND NVID ARE READ, NEVER WRITTEN
--------------------------------------

MISC0 bit 0 is left at 0, asynchronous clock mode, deliberately -- and in that
mode both registers are READ-ONLY: MVID returns what the core computes from
the input video clock, and NVID returns 0x8000
(15.4.7.55, 15.4.7.57). MVID is therefore one of the few numbers in this driver
that cannot be a register echoing our own write: it is the core measuring what
TCON_TV1 is actually feeding it. The driver converts it back to kHz and refuses
to enable the stream if it is more than 1 % from the mode, and checks NVID
against its constant on the way.

⚠ That also corrects an earlier belief of this tree: that NVID silently
drops a write when the eDP_REG_PAD gates are shut. NVID is read-only in this
mode; the write was always a no-op, and what changed when the video clock
started was the value the core returns. The observation was right and the
explanation was not.

THE TWO ENABLES WITH NO UM ROW
-------------------------------

0x0800 and 0x0804, bit 0 each, preceded by a video soft reset (0x0090 bit 0).
The register list runs from CORE_ID at 0x00FC straight to 0x0808, so neither has
a row. They are named twice elsewhere and the two names disagree: UM 15.4.5.3
refers to the SOURCE_ENABLE and VIDEO_STREAM_ENABLE host registers as
the main link enables, while the vendor calls them
TR_VIDEO_STREAM_ENABLE and TR_SECOND_STREAM_ENABLE (trilinear_dp14.h:113-114),
i.e. it reads the second as stream #2 of a multi-stream link.

Their absence is why the first successful link training here dropped: with
training cleared and the stream not enabled the core sends idle and the sink
loses lock. Both are written, because both were set when this board produced a
picture and neither has been tested alone. ⬜ If the vendor's reading is right,
0x0804 has no business being set on a single-stream link -- and separating them
costs one write: `devmem 0x05740804 32 0`.

WHERE THE VENDOR DRIVER DIFFERS, AND WHAT WE DO INSTEAD
-------------------------------------------------------

Read from bsp/drivers/drm/sunxi_device/hardware/lowlevel_edp/trilinear_dp14/ and
sunxi_drm_edp.c. (The other low-level driver in that directory, inno_edp13, is
not built for this SoC: CONFIG_AW_DRM_TRILINEAR_EDP14=y, no INNO_EDP13 line in
any defconfig, and no object file.)

| vendor | ours |
|---|---|
| writes MVID and NVID unconditionally, as the FIRST thing it does, before it has computed the clocking mode -- with `// not sure divide 1000 or 100000, spec seems conflict` sitting above the MVID write (trilinear_dp14.c:1262-1266). In async mode both are read-only, so both writes have no effect | neither is written; MVID is read as a measurement and the stream is refused if it disagrees with the mode, NVID is checked against its constant |
| reads MVID back in one place, scales it by 1000 and prints it in sysfs as the pixel clock (:1638, sunxi_drm_edp.c:1803) -- which on an async link reports 18690 kHz for a 154000 kHz mode. It is never compared with anything | the same register, converted with the link symbol rate the way the UM defines it, and compared |
| leaves the clocking mode to the zeroed allocation: `edp_core->sync_clock` is declared, read twice and assigned nowhere, so kzalloc decides the clocking mode of every link it drives (sunxi_edp.h:444, :1274, :1320). Same for `interlace` -> 0x0860 always 0, and `colormetry` -> MISC0 bit 4 always BT601 | async chosen deliberately, with the consequence for MVID stated |
| never reads SRC0_USER_FIFO_STATUS, SRC0_USER_FRAMING_STATUS or SRC0_INPUT_STATUS. The macros exist and the tree-wide grep for each returns only its own #define line | all three read out, and INPUT_STATUS sampled eight times because bit 2 is low during blanking |
| never programs SRC0_DATA_CONTROL either -- also #define-only | not programmed, but checked against the TU-size constraint the UM states |
| `edp_video_stream_enable()` sets 0x0804 and its disable line is commented out (:908, :914), so the second stream stays enabled for the lifetime of the driver after any modeset | both enables cleared on disable, in reverse order |
| its eDP path does a video soft reset before enabling the stream and its DisplayPort path does not (sunxi_drm_edp.c:3750-3752 against :3994-3999) -- same IP, same MSA block, two sequences | one sequence |
| `edp_hw_link_start()`'s return value discarded at both call sites (:3752, :3999) while every neighbouring call is checked | checked |
| TU arithmetic truncates rather than rounds and hides the TU size in a bare literal: `data_per_tu = (pixel_clk * 8 * bpp) / bw` where the 8 is LS_PER_TU/8000*1000 (:1195). Change LS_PER_TU and :1199 follows, :1195 does not | one division into sixteenths, and TU_SIZE is a symbol wherever it appears |
| `if (bw != 0)` with no else (:1194): a zero link bandwidth leaves TU_CONFIG at whatever it held and returns RET_OK, so the caller's error check is unreachable | a symbol count of zero, or one larger than the TU, refuses with the mode, rate, lane count and bpp in the message |
| SECONDARY_DATA_WINDOW computed by reading HSTART back out of 0x083C (:897), an undocumented ordering dependency on set_video_timings that nothing enforces or comments; and no divide-by-zero guard on the pixel clock | computed from the mode that is being programmed, in one place |
| maps eDP_REG_PAD, logs "Maybe there is no top addr for edp!" at DEBUG level when it is absent, and then dereferences NULL (sunxi_drm_edp.c:2835-2839 into trilinear_dp14.c:1215); never unmaps it (:2953). Its named macros for that window are 0x2000/0x2004 in the MAIN window, whose mapping is 0x1000 long -- dead defines that would write past the end (trilinear_dp14.h:183-185) | the window is optional and absent means a named refusal from `video`, not a NULL write; devm-managed |
| its YUV420 branch writes `SET_BITS(shift=3, width=4, val=0xc0)` with a macro that does not mask val, so 0xc0 << 3 sets bits 9-10 outside the field -- and then falls through and programs MISC0 as RGB anyway (:1286-1292) | — |
| `if ((lane_cnt < 0) || (lane_cnt > 4))` on a u32 (:868), so the first half can never be true; and when the check fires the function is void, so the caller cannot tell | — |
| `trilinear_enable()`, `trilinear_irq_enable/disable()`, `trilinear_set_lane_sw_pre()` are empty bodies registered in the ops table; `edp_controller_soft_reset()` is 100 % commented out and still called | no such slots |

AND WHAT IT GETS RIGHT, WHICH IS TAKEN

  - The MISC0 encodings, and its own reader decodes the same fields the same way
    (:1279-1307 against :1573-1630). Ported as-is.
  - USER_DATA_COUNT: two correct ceilings, `ceil(hres*bpp/8)` then
    `ceil(.../lanes)` (:1368-1369). The same expression is here.
  - HSTART and VSTART as sync-leading-edge to first-active, which is what the
    MSA fields mean (:1415, :1421).
  - POLARITY as PVSYNC << 1 | PHSYNC << 0 (:1424-1425), and the SAME two flags
    fed to TCON_TV1's TV_IO_POL -- so its two blocks cannot describe different
    modes. That is the right architecture, and `drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source` and this patch keep it by
    taking the mode from one place.
  - Refusing an unachievable mode when the symbol count exceeds the TU rather
    than failing the modeset (:1783-1826). Kept, minus its off-by-two: its own
    message says the limit is 62 where its code tests 64.
  - The enable ORDER: training, then MSA, then the transfer unit, then the video
    reset, then the stream. The transfer unit is computed from the post-training
    lane count, not the requested one. Kept, which is why `video` has two verbs.

⚠ The register-table convention for POLARITY says the opposite of what silicon
does -- it documents 0 as active high and 1 as active low (15.4.7.46) -- and then
notes that this sense is inverted relative to the user input port polarities.
Silicon settles it: a live link at a +HSync -VSync mode carries 0x1. ⬜ One
mode; the VSYNC bit has never been exercised set.

SRC0_USER_SYNC_POLARITY (0x0864) is left at its 0x0F reset and only verified.
⬜ Its derivation is genuinely open: 0x0F says all four user inputs are active
high, which does not follow from TCON_TV1's TV_IO_POL under any reading we can
justify, and the same +HSync -VSync mode is the only one anyone has run. A mode
with the other polarity moves TV_IO_POL and would say whether 0x0864 has to move
with it.

APPLY ORDER
-----------

This patch edits only drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c, which
`drm-allwinner-a733-displayport-aux` created and
`drm-a733-dp-configure-the-main-link-phy` extended, and it includes
sun60i-display.h, which
`drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source` adds -- so it must
apply after both.

The mode itself lives in sun60i-display.h rather than twice, and that is an
invariant rather than tidiness: TCON_TV1 generates the raster and the MSA tells
the sink what that raster is, and the hardware reports a disagreement between
the two as SRC0_USER_FIFO_STATUS != 0, i.e. the user FIFO reporting that
the programming does not fit the video mode. It is bring-up scaffolding with
a defined end -- when a KMS driver exists the mode comes from the connector,
which is why every function here already takes it as an argument.

WHAT IT NEEDS FROM THE DEVICE TREE
-----------------------------------

A SECOND REG WINDOW on the DisplayPort node: eDP_REG_PAD at 0x05760000, index 1.
`arm64-dts-a733-displayport-transmitter` deliberately has one window because the AUX path measurably never needs
these gates; the patch that drives pixels adds the second, exactly as predicted.
That DTS change is `arm64-dts-a733-tcon-tv1-and-the-dp-phy-pad-window`, later in the series with the pixel-clock patches, so it is not in this patch. Absent,
this driver still probes, AUX still works, and `video` refuses by name.

⚠ Their absence does not look like a missing clock gate: with them shut, fifteen
of seventeen main-stream registers read back correctly and only MVID and NVID
misbehave. They are opened FIRST here, before any MSA write, for that reason --
the by-hand run opened them last and had to re-write NVID afterwards.

⬜ One might expect both bit 0 and bit 4 of DP_VID_CLK_CFG to go to 1.
Only bit 0 does. Bit 4 is VID1_CLK_GATING, the second video source's gate; the
register reads 0x00000001 on the board once a picture is up and the vendor
writes 0x1 (trilinear_dp14.c:1216).

WHAT IT OWES
-------------

  1. THE STREAM, on a kernel with `drm-a733-tcon-tv1-timing-generator-and-video-source` and the pixel-clock DT present:

       echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5731000.lcd-controller/enable
       echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/aux_enable
       echo 0 > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/link
       echo configure > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/video
       (train the link by hand here: 4 lanes, 2700 Mb/s)
       echo stream > /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/video
       cat /sys/kernel/debug/5740000.displayport/video

     PASS: transfer unit "size 64, 27 + 6/16", data count 1440, pixel clock
     within a digit of 154000 kHz, fifo status 0 and DEN seen. Falsified by: a
     non-zero fifo status (this mode is mis-programmed), a data count that is not
     1440 at four lanes, or the measured pixel clock disagreeing with the mode.
  2. AT TWO LANES, which is the case the hardcoded script got wrong: the same
     sequence with `link` asked for 2 lanes must produce "54 + 12/16" and 2880,
     not "27 + 6/16" and 1440.
  3. A PICTURE, by eye. Nothing else proves it -- a register
     read-back alone has been mistaken for that proof before.
  4. THE 252 DECISION, checked rather than assumed: fifo and framing status zero
     with the computed window, and the picture unchanged when
     `devmem 0x05740808 32 0x62` puts the carried value back.

Build-tested only -- not hardware-proven. Applies at fuzz 0 on the full
series; builds W=1 warning-free; checkpatch --strict reports 0 errors,
0 warnings, 0 checks.

Assisted-by: Claude

diff --git a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/sun60i/sun60i-dp.c
@@ -53,6 +53,7 @@
 #include <linux/i2c.h>
 #include <linux/io.h>
 #include <linux/iopoll.h>
+#include <linux/math64.h>
 #include <linux/minmax.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
@@ -67,6 +68,9 @@
 #include <drm/display/drm_dp.h>
 #include <drm/display/drm_dp_helper.h>
 #include <drm/drm_edid.h>
+#include <drm/drm_modes.h>
+
+#include "sun60i-display.h"
 
 /* A733 user manual 15.4.7 register descriptions. Only the AUX block and the core id
  * are named here; the rest of the 4 KiB window belongs to link training and
@@ -138,6 +142,136 @@ static const unsigned int sun60i_dp_link_rates[] = { 810000, 540000, 270000, 162
 
 #define SUN60I_DP_RATE_TO_MBPS(r)	((unsigned int)(r) / 100)
 
+/*
+ * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ * The main video stream, UM 15.4.5.3 and the register tables 15.4.7.14 to
+ * 15.4.7.64
+ * ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
+ */
+#define DP_TRANSMITTER_OUTPUT_ENABLE	0x0080		/* 15.4.7.14 */
+#define  TX_OUTPUT_ENABLE		BIT(0)
+#define DP_SOFT_RESET			0x0090		/* 15.4.7.15, write-only */
+#define  SOFT_RESET_VIDEO		BIT(0)
+#define  SOFT_RESET_LINK		BIT(4)
+#define DP_INPUT_SOURCE_ENABLE		0x0094		/* 15.4.7.16 */
+#define  INPUT_SOURCE_ENABLE_SST	BIT(0)		/* "only bit 0 is valid" */
+
+/*
+ * ⚠ 0x0800 and 0x0804 have NO ROW in the UM's register list: 15.4.6 runs from
+ * CORE_ID at 0x00FC straight to SRC0_SECONDARY_DATA_WINDOW at 0x0808. They are
+ * named twice elsewhere and the two names do not agree, so both are recorded:
+ * UM 15.4.5.3 refers to the SOURCE_ENABLE and VIDEO_STREAM_ENABLE host
+ * registers as the main link enables, while the vendor calls
+ * them TR_VIDEO_STREAM_ENABLE and TR_SECOND_STREAM_ENABLE
+ * (trilinear_dp14.h:113-114) -- i.e. it reads the second one as stream #2 of a
+ * multi-stream link.
+ *
+ * ⬜ If the vendor's reading is right, 0x0804 has no business being set on an
+ * SST link at all -- and it sets it unconditionally, then never clears it,
+ * because its own disable line is commented out (trilinear_dp14.c:908, :914).
+ * Both are written here because both were set when this board produced a
+ * picture and neither has been tested alone. Separating them costs one write on
+ * a live link: `devmem 0x05740804 32 0`.
+ */
+#define DP_SRC0_ENABLE			0x0800
+#define  SRC0_ENABLE			BIT(0)
+#define DP_SRC0_STREAM_ENABLE		0x0804
+#define  SRC0_STREAM_ENABLE		BIT(0)
+
+#define DP_SRC0_SECONDARY_DATA_WINDOW	0x0808		/* 15.4.7.40 */
+#define  SECONDARY_DATA_WINDOW		GENMASK(11, 0)
+#define DP_SRC0_INPUT_STATUS		0x080c		/* 15.4.7.41, read-only */
+#define  INPUT_STATUS_ODDEVEN		BIT(3)
+#define  INPUT_STATUS_DEN		BIT(2)
+#define  INPUT_STATUS_HSYNC		BIT(1)
+#define  INPUT_STATUS_VSYNC		BIT(0)
+#define DP_SRC0_DATA_CONTROL		0x0810		/* 15.4.7.42 */
+#define  DATA_CONTROL_ACCUM_DELAY	GENMASK(14, 8)
+#define  DATA_CONTROL_FIFO_DEPTH	GENMASK(5, 0)
+#define DP_SRC0_MAIN_STREAM_HTOTAL	0x0820		/* 15.4.7.44 */
+#define DP_SRC0_MAIN_STREAM_VTOTAL	0x0824		/* 15.4.7.45 */
+#define DP_SRC0_MAIN_STREAM_POLARITY	0x0828		/* 15.4.7.46 */
+#define  MAIN_STREAM_VSYNC_POLARITY	BIT(1)
+#define  MAIN_STREAM_HSYNC_POLARITY	BIT(0)
+#define DP_SRC0_MAIN_STREAM_HSWIDTH	0x082c		/* 15.4.7.47 */
+#define DP_SRC0_MAIN_STREAM_VSWIDTH	0x0830		/* 15.4.7.48 */
+#define DP_SRC0_MAIN_STREAM_HRES	0x0834		/* 15.4.7.49 */
+#define DP_SRC0_MAIN_STREAM_VRES	0x0838		/* 15.4.7.50 */
+#define DP_SRC0_MAIN_STREAM_HSTART	0x083c		/* 15.4.7.51 */
+#define DP_SRC0_MAIN_STREAM_VSTART	0x0840		/* 15.4.7.52 */
+#define DP_SRC0_MISC0			0x0844		/* 15.4.7.53 */
+#define  MISC0_BIT_DEPTH		GENMASK(7, 5)
+#define   MISC0_BPC_6			0
+#define   MISC0_BPC_8			1
+#define   MISC0_BPC_10			2
+#define   MISC0_BPC_12			3
+#define   MISC0_BPC_16			4
+#define  MISC0_COMPONENT_FORMAT		GENMASK(2, 1)
+#define   MISC0_FORMAT_RGB		0
+#define  MISC0_SYNCHRONOUS_CLOCK	BIT(0)
+#define DP_SRC0_MISC1			0x0848		/* 15.4.7.54 */
+#define DP_SRC0_MVID			0x084c		/* 15.4.7.55 */
+#define  MVID_MASK			GENMASK(23, 0)
+#define DP_SRC0_TU_CONFIG		0x0850		/* 15.4.7.56 */
+#define  TU_CONFIG_FRAC_SYMBOLS		GENMASK(27, 24)
+/*
+ * ⚠ The UM contradicts itself on the next field and neither statement can be
+ * cited alone: 15.4.5.3's prose puts SYMBOLS_PER_TU at [22:16],
+ * the register table 15.4.7.56 at [23:16]. It cannot matter at any legal
+ * setting -- the field's own maximum is 64, seven bits -- but a driver that
+ * quoted one and not the other would look settled where it is not.
+ */
+#define  TU_CONFIG_SYMBOLS		GENMASK(23, 16)
+#define  TU_CONFIG_SIZE			GENMASK(15, 0)
+#define DP_SRC0_NVID			0x0854		/* 15.4.7.57 */
+#define  NVID_ASYNC_CONSTANT		0x8000
+#define DP_SRC0_USER_PIXEL_COUNT	0x0858		/* 15.4.7.58 */
+#define DP_SRC0_USER_DATA_COUNT		0x085c		/* 15.4.7.59 */
+#define  USER_DATA_COUNT_MASK		GENMASK(17, 0)
+#define DP_SRC0_MAIN_STREAM_INTERLACED	0x0860		/* 15.4.7.60 */
+#define DP_SRC0_USER_SYNC_POLARITY	0x0864		/* 15.4.7.61 */
+#define  USER_SYNC_POLARITY_RESET	0x0f
+#define DP_SRC0_USER_CONTROL		0x0868		/* 15.4.7.62 */
+#define  USER_CONTROL_SPARSE_MODE	BIT(0)		/* the A733 user manual */
+#define DP_SRC0_USER_FIFO_STATUS	0x086c		/* 15.4.7.63, read-only */
+#define  USER_FIFO_UNDERFLOW		BIT(1)
+#define  USER_FIFO_OVERFLOW		BIT(0)
+#define DP_SRC0_USER_FRAMING_STATUS	0x0870		/* 15.4.7.64, clear-on-read */
+#define  FRAMING_VBI_TIMING_ERROR	BIT(1)
+#define  FRAMING_DATA_UNDERFLOW		BIT(0)
+
+/* eDP_REG_PAD, the second register window. UM 15.4.8. */
+#define PAD_DP_LNK_CLK_CFG		0x0000		/* the A733 user manual */
+#define  LNK_CLK_GATING			BIT(0)
+#define PAD_DP_VID_CLK_CFG		0x0004		/* the A733 user manual */
